[REFERENCE MATERIALS]

@Image 1 is the sole reference for the city, sky, and world design of Scene 5. Strictly preserve the floating container city above the clouds, industrial buildings, watchtowers, antennas, elevated platforms, bright cyan-blue sky, pink horizon, massive coral-red sun, and restrained cyan rim glow. Multiple distant patrol quadcopters may appear only as small background silhouettes. They must not approach the protagonist.

@Image 2 is the sole identity and costume reference for the female protagonist. The portrait, front, back, side, and expression views all depict the same person, not multiple characters. Only one female protagonist may appear. Strictly preserve her dark brown skin, brown eyes, short thick white dreadlocks, blue markings across her eyes and cheeks, pink scarf, white-and-orange futuristic armor, light beige utility pants, single mechanical prosthetic arm, facial identity, and body proportions. She does not wear a helmet. Do not mirror the character or switch the prosthetic arm to the opposite side.

@Image 3 is the sole reference for the close escort robot. All views show the same robot, not multiple robots. Only one close escort robot may appear. Strictly preserve its white spherical body, orange armor panels, central cyan glowing eye, two side ducted fans, two small mechanical arms, and rounded proportions. It must remain visually distinct from the distant patrol quadcopters.

@Image 4 is the sole appearance and structural reference for the motorcycle. Only one motorcycle may appear in the video. Strictly preserve its white-and-magenta armored body, transparent pink windscreen, cyan LED headlights, cyan illuminated wheel rims, thick black treaded tires, exposed black mechanical frame, metallic engine, front and rear suspension, twin exhaust system, dark gray seat, and futuristic off-road racing proportions.

Strictly preserve the motorcycle’s silhouette, front and rear wheel sizes, wheelbase, armor segmentation, windscreen angle, headlight shape, frame position, exhaust placement, and primary color relationships from @Image 4. Do not redesign it as a hovering car, aircraft, scooter, or another vehicle category.

Preserve the pink side number “079” in its original armor-panel position whenever possible. Do not change “079” into another number. Do not generate corrupted text, random letters, or fictional brand logos. If the model cannot reproduce the number reliably, keep the armor area clean so that an accurate “079” can be added during post-production.

@Video 1 is used only as the reference for the riding performance, motorcycle motion, flight trajectory, camera angles, composition changes, editing order, movement through the city, eye close-up, and final scanline transition.

@Video 1 must not override or redesign the motorcycle from @Image 4. If compressed frames, blurred structures, or colors in @Video 1 conflict with @Image 4, @Image 4 always has the highest priority. Use the video only for motion, speed, direction, timing, and camera language. Do not copy blurred text or structural errors from the video.

@Audio 1 is the original sound extracted from @Video 1. From 0 to 6.06 seconds, preserve its rhythm, timbre, dynamic changes, spatial quality, and synchronization with the actions. From 6.06 to 8 seconds, naturally extend the final sound texture to support the eye close-up and horizontal scanline transition. Do not end in sudden silence. Do not add dialogue or unrelated music.

[MOTORCYCLE CONSISTENCY RULES]

The motorcycle in every shot must be the same motorcycle from @Image 4. Frontal, side, rear, top-down, and airborne views may change only the camera angle, never the vehicle identity, structure, or colors.

In the ultra-wide launch shot, preserve the white-and-magenta armor, transparent pink windscreen, cyan headlights, and cyan illuminated wheel rims from @Image 4.

In the heroic side-profile flight shot, clearly preserve the complete side silhouette, front-to-rear wheel proportions, engine structure, twin exhausts, and “079” number area from @Image 4.

In the rear aerial dive shot, preserve the twin exhaust placement, rear-wheel width, seat shape, and rear armor structure.

Do not deform the wheels, turn the tires oval, alter the wheelbase, melt the frame, add random armor panels, float mechanical parts, change the number of wheels, swap the front and rear wheels, remove the windscreen, change the colors, merge the rider with the motorcycle, or transform the motorcycle into a different model between shots.
